okay today i'm going to talk to you  about halogens  so halogens are group 7 of the periodic  table so they're found towards  the right-hand side this means because  they're in group 7 it shows that they  have  seven electrons in the outer shell  so the different halogens so group seven  elements are fluorine chlorine bromine  iodine and astatine  we need to know a few things about these  different elements  so first of all we need to know about  the appearance of chlorine bromine and  iodine  so at room temperature chlorine  appears like a green as a green gas  whereas bromine so if i show bromine  just in a little  uh test tube here so bromine  is a brown liquid now the way  nice easy way to remember that is br  starts for bromine  and b are brown  also it's gives should give off an  orange vapor at room temperature as well  now iodine iodine here  is a dark gray  solid so as you can see  as you go down group seven down your  halogens  the boiling and melting point increases  because this one these ones are gases  bromine is a liquid and these two are  both solid  now iodine also when heated  gives off a purple vapor so those are  the appearances of each of those  different  halogens at room temperature the next  important thing to know about halogens  is they are diatomic  now what that means is they go around in  pairs  so chlorine is cl2  two chlorine atoms covalently bonded  together to form a chlorine molecule  bromine is br2 and  iodine is i2  fluorine would be f2 astatine at2  so as you can see they're diatomic dye  meaning two atoms  so two atoms covalently bonded together  the next thing we need to know about  halogens is their reactivity  because there's a trend as you go down  the group  at the top of the group is the most  reactive one in the form of fluorine  this is the  opposite pattern to that of alkali  metals  it's completely the reversed um  pattern so this for halogens the least  reactive  a right down at the bottom what i'm  going to do now is explain  why fluorine is the most reactive and  why it gets  less reactive the further and further  you go down the table  so i'm going to draw fluorine which has  nine electrons  so there's the first shell one two  three four five six seven eight  nine so as we can see because it's in  group seven  i have one two three four five six seven  electrons  in the outer shell so for halogens  to get a full outer shell they are  looking  to gain one electron  so they need one more electron to  complete their outer shell  so when they're forming an ion they need  to attract  electrons from another  element from another atom so  the reason why fluorine is the most  reactive  is due to the fact it has a very small  atomic radius the atomic radius is the  distance from the center of the atom to  the outer electrons  so as you can see fluorine's atomic  radius  is quite small now the reason why that  makes it reactive is because of this  in the center in the nucleus we have  protons which are positive and they  attract negative electrons from  neighboring atoms  so if i've got a very small atomic  radius  there's going to be more attraction on  these nearby electrons  therefore fluorine is quite reactive  so let's say we've managed to get that  extra electron  so we now have a full outer shell but  i'll just do it in a different color to  show it's come from a different atom  this is what we we have we have fluorine  reacting or gaining an electron  to form f minus  so all halogens form one minus ions  like this so let's just have a quick  look at chlorine  in comparison so chlorine has 17  electrons  so let me just draw this quickly so what  we should be able to see  in a second is the fact that  chlorine has a bigger  atomic radius the distance between the  center of the of the atom  and the outer electrons is much bigger  which means  if the positive protons are attracting  neighboring electrons they're not going  to have  they're not going to be the same degree  of attraction  as with fluorine so as you go down the  group  and the atomic radius gets bigger and  bigger and bigger  there's less and less attraction on  these neighboring or these nearby  electrons  therefore it becomes less and less  reactive  so finally just to show that this time  we have chlorine  gaining an electron so if it was to gain  an electron  we would now have  cl one minus ion and that  is the introduction to halogens thank  you
 

